  const [data, setData] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetchData();
  }, []);

  const fetchData = async () => {
    const response = await fetch('https://my-api.com/data');
    const data = await response.json();
    setData(data);
  };

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>Data: {data.length}</Text>
      {data.map((item) => (
        <Text key={item.id}>{item.name}</Text>
      ))}
    </View>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;
```
In this example, the component uses the `useState` and `useEffect` hooks to manage state and fetch data. The `useState` hook is used to initialize the `data` state variable as an empty array, and the `useEffect` hook is used to fetch data when the component mounts.

The `fetchData` function is an asynchronous function that uses the `fetch` API to fetch data from an external API. The response is parsed as JSON and stored in the `data` state variable.

The component uses a `View` component to render the data to the screen, and a `Text` component to display the length of the data array. The data is rendered using a `Map` function, which iterates over the `data` array and renders a `Text` component for each item.

